    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d100">

                <pb facs="0340" n="300" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v09p1/pg300"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">780.5/10–2452: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 100<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Iraq (Berry) to the
                        Department of State</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d100-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Baghdad</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1952-10-24T15:00:00+03:00">October 24, 1952—3
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>531. Re London’s 2304 Oct 20.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d100-f2"/> Emb doubts that
                    Iraq can or shld be split off from other Arab states in regard participation in
                    MEDO or that Turks can be useful broach this subj to Iraq Govt. It is probably
                    true that Regent,<anchor n="3" xml:id="d100-f3"/> with his personal pro-Western
                    inclinations, displayed favorable attitude in discussing MEDO with Turk PriMin
                    in London, but bringing Iraq Govt as whole to make break with other Arab states
                    not only difficult but probably also inadvisable from standpoint Iraqs internal
                    stability. In any case present caretaker Iraq Govt in no position to make
                    decision.</p>
                <p>If MEDO made sufficiently attractive Iraqis cld be persuaded use their influence
                    in discussion with other Arab states to bring about collective Arab decision
                    participate in MEDO but in this Emb’s view Egypt must also be persuaded before
                    positive Arab action likely.</p>
                <p>Anti-Turk sentiment presently so high here that Emb doubts if Turk intervention
                    wld be fruitful. Turk has no Amb here and intervention by Chargé ahead of Brit
                    and US Amb might not only create suspicion concerning Turk motive but also give
                    impression that US and UK were trying to get out-from-under.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Berry</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d100-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d100-f1-content">
                <p>Repeated to Amman, London, Ankara, Paris, Beirut, Damascus, Cairo, and Jidda.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d100-f2" n="2" xml:id="d100-f2-content">
                <p><ref target="#d97">Document 97</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d100-f3" n="3" xml:id="d100-f3-content">
                <p>Crown Prince Abdul Ilah, uncle of King Faisal II who was a minor.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
